    <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Foundation</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#shivaji"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shivaji.jpg" alt="Shivaji" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#shivaji">Shivaji</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shivaji_British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Maratha Empire emerged on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a> in the mid-17th century as a regional power challenging the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a> and, in time, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. Its origins are closely tied to the political turmoil of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan">Deccan</a>, where the sultanates of Ahmadnagar and Bijapur were declining under Mughal pressure, and to the rise of local warrior and landholding families known as Marathas. The founding figure, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shivaji">Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj</a> (1630–1680), son of the Bijapur commander Shahaji Bhonsle, exploited the rugged terrain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> and a keen grasp of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare to seize strategically significant forts such as Torna, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rajgad">Rajgad</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#raigad">Raigad</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#coronation-of-shivaji">coronation of Shivaji</a> at Raigad in 1674 formally asserted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, establishing a hereditary monarchy and a political identity rooted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marathi">Marathi</a> culture and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hinduism">Hindu</a> kingship. His statecraft drew on peasant support, the loyalty of Maratha warrior families and shifting alliances, including a pact with the Qutb Shahi sultanate of Golconda in 1677, allowing a relatively small polity to contest Mughal authority. The state survived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s long Deccan war after Shivaji's death, and by the early 18th century the Mughals' overextension and the fracturing of the Deccan had opened the way for Maratha expansion across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#dabhol"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dabhol.jpg" alt="Dabhol" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#dabhol">Dabhol</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dabhol_Graveyard_Mosque.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nomaanmetkar, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Maratha socio-economic system combined a militarised aristocracy with an agrarian base. Society was organised hierarchically, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chhatrapati">Chhatrapati</a> and his council (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ashta-pradhan">Ashta Pradhan</a>) at the apex, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardar">sardars</a> (noble commanders), deshmukhs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#patil">patils</a> (district and village headmen) and the peasants who formed the agrarian backbone. The population was predominantly Marathi-speaking and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hinduism">Hindu</a>, with Muslim, tribal and other communities integrated through trade, taxation or military service. Agriculture was the principal economic activity, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a> grown on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a> and jowar, bajra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sugarcane">sugarcane</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan-plateau">Deccan plateau</a>, while the forests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timber">timber</a> and other products. Beyond their own lands the Marathas levied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chauth">chauth</a>, a quarter of the land revenue, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardeshmukhi">sardeshmukhi</a>, a further tenth claimed as a hereditary right, financing both administration and military campaigns. Internal trade flowed along <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan">Deccan</a> routes, while Konkan ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dabhol">Dabhol</a> linked the Marathas to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> trade in cotton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spice-trade">spices</a> and the horses essential for cavalry. Revenue collection was largely decentralised, empowering local sardars and revenue farmers while maintaining allegiance to the centre and enabling rapid mobilisation of fiscal and human resources.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Military</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#aurangzeb"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aurangzeb.jpg" alt="Aurangzeb" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aurangzeb-portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Darbarscene.jpg: Cordanrad derivative work: Rani nurmai (talk), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Maratha military combined infantry, cavalry and, later, artillery in a flexible and highly mobile system suited to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan">Deccan</a>'s terrain. Cavalry formed the backbone of Maratha power, with light horsemen capable of rapid raids and ambushes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shivaji">Shivaji</a>'s innovations included the extensive use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hill-fort">hill forts</a> as defensive anchors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ganimi-kava">Ganimi Kava</a>) and decentralised command, allowing local leaders to respond swiftly to threats. The Marathas also built an effective navy, with sea forts such as Sindhudurg and Vijaydurg, to defend the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a> against the Portuguese and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siddis">Siddis</a> of Janjira; under Kanhoji Angre in the early 18th century it challenged even the English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch">Dutch</a>. Notable campaigns under Shivaji included the capture of Torna (1646), the killing of the Bijapur general Afzal Khan at Pratapgad (1659) and the raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#surat">Surat</a> (1664). After the death of Shivaji and the execution of his son Sambhaji in 1689, the state survived a long war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> and re-emerged under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peshwa">Peshwas</a>, the hereditary chief ministers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#balaji-vishwanath">Peshwa Balaji Vishwanath</a> (1713–1720) and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baji-rao-i">Baji Rao I</a> (1720–1740) drove expansion north into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malwa">Malwa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gujarat">Gujarat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bundelkhand">Bundelkhand</a>, defeating the Nizam at Palkhed (1728) and Bhopal (1737) and raiding the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a> in 1737. Military power underpinned political legitimacy, revenue extraction and territorial expansion, making the Marathas a dominant force in 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ashta-pradhan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashta-pradhan.jpg" alt="Ashta Pradhan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ashta-pradhan">Ashta Pradhan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Daniell,_Sir_Charles_Warre_Malet,_Concluding_a_Treaty_in_1790_in_Durbar_with_the_Peshwa_of_the_Maratha_Empire.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomas Daniell, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Maratha state developed a sophisticated administrative and legal framework under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shivaji">Shivaji</a>, later transformed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peshwa">Peshwas</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ashta-pradhan">Ashta Pradhan</a>, or council of eight ministers, oversaw military affairs, finance, records, foreign relations, justice and religious matters, each with defined responsibilities, an early form of bureaucratic specialisation. Shivaji favoured paying officials in cash rather than with hereditary land grants, limiting the power of local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#magnates">magnates</a>. Districts were governed by appointed officers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#subedar">subedars</a> responsible for revenue and defence, while village assemblies (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#panchayat">panchayats</a>) settled disputes according to customary law. In the 18th century real power passed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chhatrapati">Chhatrapati</a> at Satara to the Peshwa's administration at Pune, which supervised a confederacy of semi-autonomous chiefs, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gaekwads">Gaekwads</a> of Baroda, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#holkars">Holkars</a> of Indore, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#scindias">Scindias</a> of Gwalior and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bhonsles">Bhonsles</a> of Nagpur, who collected revenue and maintained armies while acknowledging the Peshwa's leadership and the Chhatrapati's formal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. This decentralised yet coherent structure allowed the Marathas to control territory spanning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan">Deccan</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> and much of the north, balancing local autonomy with central oversight.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#delhi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/delhi.jpg" alt="Delhi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jama_Masjid_2011.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marcin Białek, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Marathas broke Mughal supremacy and became the principal political and military power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> by the mid-18th century, when their armies reached Attock on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indus">Indus</a> in 1758. They carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marathi">Marathi</a> language, culture and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hinduism">Hindu</a> ritual patronage across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan">Deccan</a> and into northern and eastern territories. Maratha ascendancy reshaped the political map, compelling alliances, tribute arrangements and settlements with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rajputs">Rajput</a> states, the Nawabs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bengal">Bengal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sikhism">Sikh</a> chiefs, and under Mahadji Scindia the Marathas controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a> and the Mughal emperor in the 1770s and 1780s. Their control of key routes linked regional economies from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gujarat">Gujarat</a> to Bengal, while their tribute system stimulated trade in arms, horses, textiles and grain. European powers, especially the British and Portuguese, dealt with the Marathas both diplomatically and militarily, recognising their control of central and western Indian trade corridors. Their legacy includes hill and sea forts, temples and ghats, as well as successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#princely-state">princely states</a> such as Baroda, Gwalior and Indore that endured until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indian-independence">Indian independence</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#third-battle-of-panipat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat.jpg" alt="Third Battle of Panipat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Third_battle_of_Panipat_13_January_1761.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Maratha decline stemmed from both internal fragmentation and external pressure. Expansion continued under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peshwa">Peshwa</a> Balaji Baji Rao after 1740, but rivalry among the great chiefs, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gaekwads">Gaekwads</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#holkars">Holkars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#scindias">Scindias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bhonsles">Bhonsles</a>, increasingly weakened central authority. Conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Abdali</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#punjab">Punjab</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> culminated in the catastrophic defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a> in 1761, which inflicted heavy military and human losses and disrupted revenue systems. Although recovery followed under Peshwa Madhav Rao I and Mahadji Scindia, succession disputes in Pune and rivalry among the chiefs left the confederacy divided. From the late 18th century the Marathas were drawn into conflict with the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-india-company">East India Company</a> in the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anglo-maratha-wars">Anglo-Maratha Wars</a> (1775–1818). Defeats such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-assaye">Battle of Assaye</a> (1803) cost them extensive territory, and the final war ended in 1818 with the surrender of Peshwa Baji Rao II and the abolition of the Peshwa's office, marking the end of Maratha political independence. The Maratha legacy nevertheless persisted in the regional politics, social structures and military traditions of western and central India.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary"><em>The Maratha Empire stands as the power that broke Mughal supremacy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, building from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shivaji">Shivaji</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hill-fort">hill forts</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare a confederacy that dominated eighteenth-century India until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anglo-maratha-wars">Anglo-Maratha Wars</a> ended its independence.</em></p>

